  rather than write a spec. One thing needs deciding first.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the form has no user-id field, so nothing in the current flow can supply a [38;5;153muserId[39m. Whatever we pick here
  becomes [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's signature and the thing the submit handler has to produce, so it's worth a sentence before I write
  it.

  - [1mClient-generated tracking id[0m — the handler creates an id (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) per login attempt and passes it
    in. Buys you a correlatable handle for logs immediately with no backend work; costs you that it identifies an
    [3mattempt[0m, not a person, so it can't answer "which account logged in" later. Cheap to change.
  - [1mCaller-supplied account id[0m — the handler passes an id it got from somewhere else (a stored session, an existing user
    record). Buys a real identity in the log line; costs you that there is no such source in this app today, so the
    handler would pass [38;5;153mnull[39m/[38;5;153mundefined[39m until one exists. Cheap to change.
  - [1mServer-assigned id echoed back[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its two params and the returned object carries the id the API
    assigns. Buys the only id that's actually authoritative about who logged in; costs you that it isn't the parameter
    you asked for, and the stub returns a fixed shape today so it's a placeholder until the real POST exists. Changing
    this later means changing the return contract, not just a call site.

  [1mMy recommendation: caller-supplied account id, as an optional third parameter[0m ([38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId = [39m
  [38;5;153mnull)[39m). It's literally what you asked for, it keeps the existing call working, and it doesn't invent an identity
  scheme that pretends to be more than a correlation token. If the intent is really "tag each attempt in the logs," the
  client-generated id is the honest version of that instead.
